{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`LLMConfig` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:473`) exposes `base_url` and `api_key` but no way to\nattach request headers, so an operator behind a gateway cannot label rebar's gate calls.\nAdding a raw dict is unsafe as-is: both vendor SDKs merge caller headers last, so a\ncaller-supplied `Authorization` silently displaces the real credential. This ticket adds the\nconfiguration surface and the validation that makes it safe, without yet sending anything.\n\n## Approach\n\nAdd `headers: dict[str, str] = field(default_factory=dict)` to `LLMConfig`, resolved in\n`from_env` by the same three-layer precedence `mcp_servers` already uses at\n`src/rebar/llm/config.py:574-594` (CLI `-c llm.headers=<json>` > `REBAR_LLM_HEADERS` JSON >\nthe `[tool.rebar.llm]` file table). Reusing that resolution shape keeps one documented\nprecedence story rather than inventing a second.\n\n**Precedence only — deliberately not error handling.** `mcp_servers` silently degrades a\nmalformed JSON payload to `{}` (`src/rebar/llm/config.py:582-583` and `:593-594`). `headers`\ndoes **not** copy that: a malformed JSON payload in any layer raises `LLMConfigError` naming\nthe offending layer. Silently dropping headers would leave an operator with an unattributed\ngateway and no signal that their configuration was discarded, and it would contradict the\nfail-loud rule applied to an unset `${env:VAR}` below. The whole surface gets one fail-loud\nstory.\n\nPut the value grammar and validation in a new leaf module `src/rebar/llm/headers.py`, so\n`config.py` (currently near the module-size policy's comfort range) does not absorb it and\nthe substitution logic is unit-testable without constructing a config.\n\nValue grammar: a literal string, or a string containing `${env:VAR}` references resolved at\nconfig-resolution time. `${env:...}` uses single braces with an explicit namespace because\n`${{ ... }}` is already claimed by the workflow DSL and carries an injection guard\n(`src/rebar/llm/workflow/lint_refs.py:5-14`); reusing it would collide with a linter whose\nthreat model is different. An unset referenced variable is a hard error, matching the\n`REBAR_LLM_CONFIG_FILE` rule that a missing path never silently degrades\n(`src/rebar/llm/config.py:19-21`).\n\n**The `${...}` grammar is closed and fail-loud.** Exactly two namespaces exist, `env` and\n`run`, and `headers.py` owns that closed set — including the `${run:...}` key vocabulary\n(`trace_id`, `ticket_id`, `operation`) as a module constant, so the grammar is self-contained\nand does not depend on the ticket that supplies those values. Any `${` begins a placeholder;\nthere is no escape for a literal `${`, because no legitimate gateway header value contains one.\nEvery occurrence is dispositioned at config-resolution time:\n\n- `${env:VAR}` — resolved now; an unset variable is a hard error.\n- `${run:KEY}` — left unresolved (its value exists only inside a gate run), but `KEY` is\n  checked against the closed vocabulary now, so a typo fails at configuration time rather than\n  mid-run.\n- An unrecognized namespace (`${foo:bar}`), a missing colon (`${envVAR}`), or an unclosed\n  `${...}` — a hard `LLMConfigError`. Never a silently-accepted literal: passing the\n  placeholder text through as a header value would be a silent misconfiguration in a feature\n  whose purpose is attribution.\n\n**Ordering is part of the contract: substitute first, then validate.** Every check below runs\non the **fully resolved** value, never on the literal containing a `${env:...}` reference.\nValidating first would let an environment variable whose value contains CR/LF carry an injected\nheader past the check that exists to stop it — for example a configured value of\n`${env:GATEWAY_TAG}` where `GATEWAY_TAG` is `foo\\r\\nInjected: bar` passes a check applied to\nthe literal, and substitution then injects the header.\n\nValidation rejects, with a named `LLMConfigError`:\n- header names in the denied set `authorization`, `x-api-key`, `cookie`, `host`,\n  `proxy-authorization` (case-insensitive) — these forge or displace authentication;\n- header names outside RFC 7230 token characters;\n- values containing CR, LF, or NUL.\n\nA denylist rather than an allowlist: arbitrary gateway header names are the point of the\nfeature, so an allowlist would defeat it. The threat being closed is credential displacement\nand protocol injection, both of which are closed sets.\n\nValue validation therefore runs on the value after `${env:...}` substitution, with any\n`${run:...}` placeholder still present as literal placeholder text — which contains no control\ncharacters, so everything knowable at configuration time is checked. The delivery ticket\nre-runs the same validation after it substitutes `${run:...}`, closing the same gap on that\nlater seam.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/llm/config.py` — the `headers` field and its three-layer resolution in `from_env`.\n- `src/rebar/llm/headers.py` (new) — grammar, `${env:...}` resolution, name/value validation.\n- `tests/unit/test_llm_headers.py` (new) — the tests below.\n\n## Testing\n\nTier: in-process unit tests against the real functions — the mechanism is pure string\nresolution and validation with no boundary to cross, so no higher tier is warranted.\n\n- Happy path: a literal header and an `${env:VAR}` header both resolve, the latter from a\n  monkeypatched environment.\n- Failure paths: each denied header name; a name with an invalid character; values containing\n  CR, LF and NUL; an `${env:VAR}` naming an unset variable; a malformed JSON\n  payload in the env layer and in the CLI layer.\n- Grammar-disposition paths: `${foo:bar}`, `${envVAR}`, `${en:VAR}`, an unclosed `${env:VAR`,\n  and `${run:bogus}` each raise; a well-formed `${run:trace_id}` survives config resolution\n  unresolved.\n- Injection-via-indirection path: a header value of `${env:VAR}` where `VAR` resolves to a\n  string containing CR, LF or NUL is rejected — the ordering regression test.\n- Negative control: a header named `x-authorization-context` is accepted, proving the denylist\n  matches exact names and does not substring-match legitimate ones.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `LLMConfig.headers` defaults to an empty dict, so an unconfigured deployment is\n      byte-unchanged; checked by `test_headers_default_empty` asserting\n      `LLMConfig.from_env().headers == {}` with no relevant env set.\n- [ ] Headers resolve from all three layers with CLI > env > file precedence; checked by a\n      test setting all three and asserting the CLI value wins, mirroring the `mcp_servers`\n      precedence test.\n- [ ] A `${env:VAR}` value resolves from the environment; checked by a test monkeypatching\n      `VAR` and asserting the resolved header equals its value.\n- [ ] An `${env:VAR}` naming an unset variable raises `LLMConfigError` naming the variable;\n      checked by `pytest.raises` asserting the variable name appears in the message.\n- [ ] Each of `authorization`, `x-api-key`, `cookie`, `host`, `proxy-authorization` is\n      rejected case-insensitively with `LLMConfigError`; checked by a parametrized test over\n      the five names in three casings.\n- [ ] A header value containing CR, LF or NUL is rejected with `LLMConfigError`; checked by a\n      parametrized test over the three characters.\n- [ ] An unrecognized namespace, a missing colon, an unclosed placeholder, and a `${run:...}`\n      key outside the closed vocabulary each raise `LLMConfigError` rather than being accepted\n      as a literal; checked by a parametrized test over `${foo:bar}`, `${envVAR}`, `${en:VAR}`,\n      `${env:VAR`, and `${run:bogus}`.\n- [ ] A well-formed `${run:trace_id}` value survives config resolution unresolved and is not\n      rejected; checked by a test asserting the stored value still contains the placeholder.\n- [ ] A value that is clean as a literal but resolves through `${env:VAR}` to a string\n      containing CR, LF or NUL is rejected with `LLMConfigError`; checked by a parametrized\n      test monkeypatching `VAR` to each control character, proving validation runs on the\n      resolved value rather than the literal.\n- [ ] A malformed JSON payload in the env layer or the CLI layer raises `LLMConfigError`\n      naming that layer, rather than degrading to an empty dict; checked by a parametrized\n      test over both layers asserting the raise and the layer name in the message.\n- [ ] A header named `x-authorization-context` is accepted; checked by a test asserting it\n      survives validation, proving exact-name rather than substring matching."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787707701040940001,"uuid":"a6d6f4c5-01c3-40d2-ad01-5ad385aaaf2e"}